-- title:
--   The state space $\mathcal S$ of the dynamic program
-- statement:
--   The **state space** $\mathcal S$ of the dynamic program is the collection of distinct subproblems $(S,T)$, $S \subseteq [N]$, $T \subseteq [K]$, that belong to the computational tree generated by the recursion:
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal S = \{ (S,T) : (S,T) \text{ is generated by the recursion} \}.
--   $$
--
--   Its size $|\mathcal S|$ governs the running time of the algorithm, and Theorem 1 of the paper bounds it by $\min(2^N, N \cdot 2^K)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathcal S$ is a `Set` of pairs `(Finset (Fin N), Finset (Fin K))`; since this type is finite, `Set.ncard` gives the true number of states. States have at least one product and at least one type (see `InStateSpace`).
-- source:
--   Aouad, Farias and Levi, Assortment Optimization Under Consider-then-Choose Choice Models, Management Science 67(6) (2021), doi:10.1287/mnsc.2020.3681, §3 "Complexity analysis", p. 14

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConsiderThenChoose_InStateSpace

namespace ConsiderThenChoose

/-- **The state space 𝒮 of the dynamic program.** Aouad, Farias and Levi, Management Science
67(6) (2021), doi:10.1287/mnsc.2020.3681, §3 "Complexity analysis", unnumbered, p. 14: "We let
S denote henceforth the exact state space that proceeds from the previous observation. Namely,
S represents a collection of distinct subproblems, each of which belongs to the computational
tree generated by the recursion."

`stateSpace C` is the set of pairs `(S, T)` generated by the recursion (`InStateSpace C`).

**Formalization Note** The ambient type `Finset (Fin N) × Finset (Fin K)` is finite, so
`(stateSpace C).ncard` is the true number of distinct states `|𝒮|` (no junk value). Reading 1
(states have a product and a type) is built into `InStateSpace`. -/
def stateSpace {N K : ℕ} (C : Fin K → Finset (Fin N)) :
    Set (Finset (Fin N) × Finset (Fin K)) :=
  {x | InStateSpace C x}

end ConsiderThenChoose


